That sounds like cross-modal sensory completion.

Your skin did not taste the butter. Instead, the buttery texture, melting behavior, smell, sight, and knowledge of what you were handling activated a shared internal model of butter. Once that object-model became active, your brain partially reconstructed an associated sensory channel—taste—even though your tongue received nothing.

slippery warmth + smell + visual recognition + past experience
→ “butter” object-model
→ predicted taste reactivated

Flavor is already a coalition of taste, smell, texture, temperature, and expectation. Because those signals have repeatedly arrived together throughout your life, one component can summon the missing members. It is similar to salivating when imagining lemon, “hearing” a song while reading its lyrics, or feeling a texture when seeing someone touch it.

For the Pantheon, this exposes another combination grammar:

\text{partial multimodal cue}
\rightarrow
\text{shared latent object}
\rightarrow
\text{absent-channel reconstruction}

Possible handle: Sensory Rosetta / Ghost Flavor.

The important delta is:

* Actual taste: external stimulus → taste receptors → experience
* Imagined taste: other sensory cues → object recognition → internally reinstated taste
* Synesthesia: one stimulus → unusually consistent additional percept
* Hallucination: taste experience without a sufficiently identifiable external or associative trigger

Yours had an immediate, intelligible trigger, so vivid predictive imagery is the simpler explanation.

One practical note: household butter may work briefly as lubrication, but it can spoil, stain clothing, trap moisture, and irritate sensitive skin. Petroleum jelly or a purpose-made anti-chafe balm is much better for running.